# Double quotes

Write string literals with double quotes. An apostrophe inside a string is a plain `'`, never `\'`.

```js
const note = "That isn't a hand seed.";
```

```py
print(f"the hand's points")
```

Keep template literals in backticks. On Python 3.10, a double-quoted f-string cannot contain another double-quoted string. Split that f-string into double-quoted pieces instead of switching one of them back to single quotes.
